---
// Page 02 — /property-valuation-dubai/golden-visa/ · TILL · Template A
// Format per spec: explainer + eligibility checker + form.
import PageShell from '../../../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import DataTable from '../../../components/DataTable.astro';
import PartnerCard from '../../../components/PartnerCard.astro';
import FaqList from '../../../components/FaqList.astro';
import RequestForm from '../../../components/RequestForm.astro';
import ValuationCrossLink from '../../../components/ValuationCrossLink.astro';
import NeedsSource from '../../../components/NeedsSource.astro';
import { byPath } from '../../../lib/pages.js';

const page = byPath('/property-valuation-dubai/golden-visa/');

const answer =
  'The Dubai golden visa property route requires a DLD-certified valuation of AED 2 million ' +
  'or more. The certificate is issued through the Dubai Land Department, and an agent’s ' +
  'appraisal is not accepted in its place. The threshold is measured on the certified ' +
  'valuation figure rather than on the price you originally paid.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the golden visa property threshold in Dubai?',
    a:
      'AED 2 million, measured on a DLD-certified valuation of the property. The figure that ' +
      'counts is the one on the certificate, not the price on your sale and purchase ' +
      'agreement.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a valuation from an estate agent count for the golden visa?',
    a:
      'No. The valuation has to be certified through the Dubai Land Department. A brokerage ' +
      'appraisal, however well evidenced, is a commercial opinion and is not the document the ' +
      'application requires.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if my property was worth less than AED 2 million when I bought it?',
    a:
      'The threshold is assessed on the current certified valuation, so a property bought ' +
      'below AED 2 million can reach the threshold later. It works the other way too: a ' +
      'property bought above it can be certified below it in a softening market.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can two properties be combined to reach AED 2 million?',
    a:
      'Whether values from more than one property can be aggregated is decided by the visa ' +
      'authority rather than by the Land Department, and the answer has changed over time. ' +
      'Confirm the current position with GDRFA before relying on it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who issues the golden visa itself?',
    a:
      'The property valuation certificate comes from the Dubai Land Department. The visa is ' +
      'issued by the General Directorate of Residency and Foreigners Affairs, which assesses ' +
      'the application as a whole rather than the valuation alone.',
  },
];
---

<PageShell page={page} h1="Golden visa property valuation in Dubai" answer={answer} faqs={faqs}>
  <h2>What does the AED 2 million threshold actually measure?</h2>

  <p>
    It measures the certified value of the property now, not what you paid for it and not what
    an agent thinks it would fetch. That distinction decides most applications that go wrong.
    An owner who bought at AED 2.1 million in a stronger market and assumes they clear the bar
    may not, and an owner who bought at AED 1.7 million some years ago may. Neither knows until
    the certificate exists.
  </p>

  <p>
    The market context matters here rather than being decoration: H1 2026 residential
    transactions in Dubai fell 13.6% year on year, and Q2 2026 saw both prices and rents
    decline. In a market moving in that direction, the gap between what you paid and what a
    current certified valuation says has been narrowing for some owners and opening for others.
    Getting the certificate is how you find out which.
  </p>

  <h2>Which valuation is accepted, and which is not?</h2>

  <DataTable
    caption="What the golden visa application will and will not accept"
    columns={['Document', 'Who issues it', 'Accepted for the visa route']}
    rows={[
      [
        'DLD-certified valuation',
        'Dubai Land Department',
        'Yes — this is the document the route is built around',
      ],
      [
        'Brokerage appraisal or market opinion',
        'A RERA-licensed brokerage',
        'No — useful for pricing a sale, not for this',
      ],
      [
        'Online estimate or portal valuation',
        'A property portal’s automated model',
        'No',
      ],
      [
        'Sale and purchase agreement price',
        'The transaction itself',
        'No — the threshold is assessed on current certified value',
      ],
    ]}
    source="Dubai Land Department valuation certification process"
  />

  <h2>What is the rule, exactly?</h2>

  <p>
    This page quotes regulations verbatim rather than paraphrasing them, and the governing text
    for the property investor route — the article, the issuing authority and the current
    wording of the threshold — has not been sourced to a primary document yet:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="verbatim GDRFA/ICP text for the property investor route, with article number" />
  </p>

  <p>
    Until that quote is in place, treat the AED 2 million figure as the widely applied
    threshold and confirm the current position directly with GDRFA before making arrangements
    that depend on it. Residency rules in the UAE are amended more often than most published
    guides are updated, and a guide that is eighteen months old can be confidently wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>What does the certification process involve?</h2>

  <p>
    In outline: the property is inspected, the Land Department assesses it against its own
    transaction records, and a certificate is issued carrying a valuation figure and a date.
    The fee schedule and the stated turnaround are published by the Land Department and belong
    on this page as numbers:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="DLD valuation certificate fee, documents required, and stated turnaround" />
  </p>

  <p>
    The step people underestimate is the one before certification: knowing roughly where the
    property sits before paying for a certificate. If recorded transactions in your building
    are clustering well under AED 2 million, the certificate is unlikely to say otherwise, and
    it is cheaper to find that out from the transaction record first.{' '}
    <a href="/property-valuation-dubai/how-it-works/">
      How Dubai property valuation works
    </a>{' '}
    sets out how that comparison is made.
  </p>

  <h2>An eligibility check before you spend anything</h2>

  <p>Work through these in order. Any "no" is worth resolving before applying.</p>

  <ul class="two-col">
    <li>The property is completed, or its status is confirmed with the visa authority</li>
    <li>You hold the title deed, and the name on it matches the applicant</li>
    <li>Recorded transactions for comparable units in the building support AED 2 million</li>
    <li>You have a DLD-certified valuation, not a brokerage appraisal</li>
    <li>The certificate is current — an old certificate reflects an older market</li>
    <li>You have checked the present rule with GDRFA rather than with a blog</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who carries out the valuation?</h2>

  <PartnerCard />

  <ValuationCrossLink
    before="If you are not yet sure whether the property is anywhere near the threshold, the sensible first step is finding out what it is actually worth today — "
    anchor="a written valuation from transactions recorded in your building"
    after=" costs nothing and takes a day."
  />

  <FaqList faqs={faqs} />

  <RequestForm />
</PageShell>
